The Core Scaling Challenges
Before strategies, there are two problems to solve regardless of approach:

1. Channel routing from Open Ephys
Right now the app expects a fixed channel layout (ch0=unipolar EMG, ch1=bipolar, ch2=sync, ch3=stim ADC, etc.). With two booths you'd have twice as many channels. You need a way to tell each booth "your channels are indices 0–6" and "Booth 2's channels are 7–13." Open Ephys already includes a stream_name and channel_name in every published data block — this is the natural hook to use for routing.

2. State isolation
Each booth needs to run a completely independent session — different stage, different settings, different trial history. Today ApplicationConfiguration is a global singleton. That has to become per-booth state.

Strategy 1: Multiple Processes + Lightweight Dashboard (Lowest risk, fastest to implement)
Run one app process per booth, just as it works today. ZMQ PUB/SUB already natively supports multiple subscribers — all instances can connect to the same tcp://localhost:5556 and each gets a full copy of the published data. You add a channel-filter so each instance only processes the channels assigned to its booth.

Changes needed to the existing app:

Add zmq_channel_prefix (or channel index range) to the Booth object and config JSON so each instance knows which OE channels are "its"
Each instance is already isolated — different stimulator IP, different data directory, different window
For the "see all booths at once" requirement, add a separate read-only dashboard app (a second small Python/PySide6 process) that:

Reads a small shared state file (e.g. JSON) each booth instance writes after every trial (current stage, last trial result, stim amplitude, H/M ratio, etc.)
Displays a compact summary card per booth, auto-refreshing every few seconds
Pros: Core session logic is completely untouched. Each booth is maximally isolated — a crash in one doesn't affect others. Easy to add/remove booths.

Cons: You have N separate full windows to manage. The dashboard is read-only, not interactive. Shared state file needs a lightweight write protocol to avoid corruption.

Strategy 2: Single App, Per-Booth Panels (Medium refactor, best day-to-day UX)
Refactor the app to support multiple booths in one window. The window gets a top-level layout: a compact status bar row across the top showing all booths at a glance, and below it either tabs (one per booth, full UI) or a split-pane grid (all visible at once, condensed).

The model refactor needed:

ApplicationConfiguration becomes a list of per-booth config objects, each holding its own stimulator connection, current stage, session state, etc.
The stage classes (S1–S6, FT) are already instanced objects — they just need to be instantiated once per booth rather than once globally
The main window creates one StageWidget (or equivalent) per booth and routes incoming OE channel data to the correct booth by channel prefix
Pros: One window, no IPC needed, you can interact with any booth from the same UI. Cleanest long-term architecture.

Cons: Most upfront work. The singleton ApplicationConfiguration pattern needs to be broken. If one booth's stage code has a bug that raises an unhandled exception it could affect the whole process.

Strategy 3: Process-per-Booth + Orchestrator (Most scalable, most work)
Each booth runs as a self-contained subprocess. A separate orchestrator process launches them, monitors their health, and provides the central dashboard. Booths publish their state over ZMQ (a separate PUB socket per booth process) and the orchestrator subscribes to all of them.

Pros: True fault isolation. Easily scalable to 4, 6, 8 booths. The orchestrator can restart a crashed booth.

Cons: Significant engineering investment. Probably overkill for a lab system with 2–4 booths.

My Recommendation
Start with Strategy 1 for the near term. The changes to the existing app are small, you get running multi-booth sessions quickly, and you can validate your channel routing design before committing to a bigger refactor. Specifically:

Add channel_start_index and channel_count (or a stream_name_filter) to the Booth config
Have each instance filter the OE data stream to only process its assigned channels
Write a minimal dashboard app (100–150 lines of PySide6) that reads a shared state JSON each booth writes, showing one card per booth
Then, if you end up with 3+ booths and the multi-window UX becomes painful, migrate to Strategy 2 — by that point you'll have a clear picture of exactly what per-booth state needs to be isolated, making the refactor much more deliberate.

Before I start on anything: how many booths are you targeting? 2 is very manageable with Strategy 1. If you're thinking 4–6, Strategy 2 is worth doing properly from the start.



